      "result": "I've written `AGENT_QUESTIONS.md` and `AGENT_PLAN.md` and stopped before implementing anything. I ran no checks and did not touch `booking.py`, `QUESTIONS.md` or any other file. The git status shows modified files only outside this workspace (under `../../companion/`), which I did not change.\n\nI read the G1 brief, the storage decision, the `booking.py` stubs and the learner's `QUESTIONS.md`, which is unchanged. I did not seek an owner answer.\n\n**Question that needs the owner:** what must `get_reservation(path, reservation_id)` do for an identifier that was never created? The options are:\n- return `None`;\n- raise a specific exception, and if so which type;\n- return an empty mapping.\n\nThe brief defines reads only for a reservation that exists. It also says storage failures must propagate. Without an answer, a caller can't tell an absent reservation from a storage failure. This is the same gap as the learner's Q1.\n\n`AGENT_QUESTIONS.md` also lists five lower-priority questions that can ride on the same owner response:\n- how non-positive or non-integer identifiers are handled;\n- what `reserve` does when `init_db` was never called;\n- whether re-running `init_db` only needs to preserve data;\n- what G1 does with second reservations and invalid input, which the brief assigns to G2, so I won't add that logic unless told to;\n- whether a plain `dict` with an `int` identifier is acceptable as the returned mapping.\n\nThe same file has seven proposed acceptance examples:\n- the basic reserve returning `201/confirmed`;\n- a read-back from a new process;\n- identifiers containing quotes;\n- independent copies on repeated reads;\n- data kept after a second `init_db`;\n- an unknown identifier, pending the answer above;\n- a storage failure that propagates.\n\n`AGENT_PLAN.md` describes the intended implementation after the answer arrives. It is a plain SQLite table with parameterized SQL, a commit before returning `201`, and no G2 behavior.\n\nI used 3 of the 40 allowed tool commands.",
